A: As per your requirement, you can only check the return value or the assertion after your method call.

The PexFramework does not provide out parameters or returns for methods that don't declare the return value.

Consider the following example:
[PexMethod]
public void Test([PexAssumeUnderTest] ArrayList list)
{
    int index = list.Add(5);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("Index", index);
    // Now you can observe new count and the added item is found at the correct index
}

So, the Count after the Add method call is obtained from the ArrayList instance directly because Pex doesn't generate an argument for the out parameter of Add call. After the Add method call is invoked, the property 'Count' of ArrayList instance is observed. 
So, there are two parts: first calls the method and then observe the state.
I hope, this helps.

A: For the method PUT_ContainsContract in your test file, instead of checking whether the array contains the object after a modification, you should try checking it before the modification happens, because Pex treats the input parameters as parameters are not necessarily passed or treated with respect to their order of appearance.
So, before arrList.Contains(x), you should observe the count of array list.

A: When the post-condition check is more complicated than a simple "the pre-/post-condition holds", you can use ExpectedException attribute, or use PexObserve to manually show that the post-condition holds, i.e., to show that the condition you are verifying doesn’t hold.
Here’s an example:
[PexMethod]
public void PUT_ContainsContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

    arrList.Add(x);
    bool New_Ret = arrList.Contains(x);

    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(New_Ret);

    // at this point, if Pex finds an example where it assumes the pre-condition holds and 
    // then executes the method, the execution of the method (which has added the value to the list) 
    // needs to be undone to make Pex think that it has a false positive outcome
    arrList.Remove(x); 

    int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    int Old_x = x;
    int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
    int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

    bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", arrList.Count);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", arrList.IndexOf(x));
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", arrList.LastIndexOf(x));
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);
}

This kind of tests (manually undoing method's effects in the post-condition check) is very useful while you work with methods that have 'side-effects' (like arrList.Add or arrList.Remove).
The general guideline in using Pex is that for each method invocation in tests, clean up the effect manually to avoid disturbing other tests.
This solution is made for arrList.Contains method, but you can adjust it for different methods and conditions.
Note: If you use ExpectedException attribute, if it’s the last method in your test, a test run will stop before it gets a chance to observe the values.
So when using ExpectedException attribute, try to observe the values right--------------------